  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Comparing two values of a value type which does not implement IEquatable<T> for equality no longer allocates. Such values were received already boxed, unboxed, and then handed to a comparer which boxed one of them again to reach the very same comparison; they are now compared as they arrive.\nFastInvoke is faster on .NET 7 and later, where it had been calling MethodBase.Invoke instead of the delegate it emits and had been using on .NET 6 all along. Invoking a constructor now costs 6.1 nanoseconds rather than 12.1; invoking a method costs 7.8 rather than 8.6, a smaller gain because reflection is closer to the delegate for methods than for constructors. Allocation is exactly what it was.\nAdded FastInvoker, which resolves the invocation of a constructor or a method once so that repeated calls neither look it up nor build an argument array. FastInvoke does both on every call, and between them they cost more than the invocation itself; a caller which invokes the same member repeatedly should resolve one of these and keep it.",
